# --- real dataset: never commit -------------------------------------------
# The 835 downloaded assemblies and their metadata are inputs, not source.
# They stay on disk; this repository tracks code and configuration only.
#
# NOTE: no trailing slash, deliberately. A `data/` pattern with the trailing
# slash matches DIRECTORIES ONLY, so it does not match a symlink named `data`
# -- git classifies a symlink as a non-directory blob-like entry. Worktrees
# share one real dataset by symlinking `data` in, and with the slash those
# symlinks showed up as untracked noise (`?? data`) and were one `git add`
# away from being committed. Dropping the slash matches the symlink too, which
# is the actual intent: the link and the data it points at both stay untracked.
data
# The bounded REAL smoke run's isolate list -- the target of
# `cohort.subset_file` in config/science.yaml. It lives in `local/`, NOT under
# `data/`, for two reasons.
#
# 1. `data` is an UNTRACKED SYMLINK into the canonical worktree, and git refuses
#    to descend through a symlink, so no ignore rule under `data/` can be
#    verified with `git check-ignore`:
#      $ git check-ignore -q data/smoke_isolates.txt ; echo $?
#      fatal: pathspec 'data/smoke_isolates.txt' is beyond a symbolic link
#      128
#    An ignore rule nobody can check is an ignore rule nobody has checked.
#    `local/` is a REAL directory in every worktree, so its rule is checkable:
#      $ git check-ignore -v local/smoke_isolates.txt
# 2. Writing through the `data` symlink would write into the read-only canonical
#    worktree, which is exactly what that symlink exists to avoid.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, deliberately, matching the `data` and `db` rules above: a
# trailing slash matches directories only, which would leave the files inside
# visible to `git status`. The list is an INPUT nobody regenerates, so a mistaken
# add would not surface in any later diff.
local
*.fna
*.fasta
*.fa
*.fsa
assembly_data_report.jsonl
dataset_catalog.json
all_*.zip
dehydrated_download.log
nohup.out
fetch.txt
PDC_*.tsv
PDC_*.txt
.~lock.*

# --- generated outputs ------------------------------------------------------
results/*
!results/.gitkeep
reports/*
!reports/.gitkeep
versions_*.txt

# --- the HPC study directory (scripts/hpc/), from the merged runner repo ----
# These came across with pa-aeruginosa-900, which had its own .gitignore.
# Anchored to scripts/hpc/ so they cannot reach results/ above or any other
# worktree's tree. The study's own results, roster, phenotype and assemblies
# are inputs and outputs of ONE cohort: multi-gigabyte, regenerated, and never
# part of the source. Only the directory itself is tracked.
/scripts/hpc/results/*
!/scripts/hpc/results/.gitkeep
/scripts/hpc/assemblies/
/scripts/hpc/annotations/
/scripts/hpc/phenotype/
/scripts/hpc/config/samples.txt
/scripts/hpc/config/phenotype.tsv
# Scheduler debris: submit_slurm.sh writes these beside the job script.
scripts/hpc/**/slurm-*.out
scripts/hpc/**/slurm-*.err

# The live run-status log. The dashboard replays this file, so it is a large
# per-run artefact, not source. Regenerated on every run.
#
# Anchored to the repository root on purpose. Unanchored, `status/` also matched
# `dashboard/fixtures/small/bundle/04_run_info/status/`, which is a committed
# fixture, not a runtime artefact -- so git silently dropped the whole
# `04_run_info/` directory and `test_bundle_has_the_assumed_layout` failed at
# be539a6, before any of this work. A fixture must be trackable; a run log must
# not. Only the root-level one is runtime output.
/status/

# --- reference databases: provisioned out-of-band, never committed ----------
# AMRFinderPlus, VFDB, MLST, Panaroo, Bakta and the PAO1 reference all land
# here. They are multi-gigabyte, machine-specific, and pinned by accession and
# checksum in config/references.tsv rather than by content in git. Note that a
# bare `db/` was previously caught only incidentally by the `*.fasta` glob
# above; real database directories contain `db.md5`, `version.txt` and
# `latest`, which that glob does not match. As with `data` above, the trailing
# slash is omitted so that a symlinked `db` is matched too.
db
databases/
# The committed synthetic fixtures live in test_data/, but the genome FASTA
# directory is symlinked in from the real dataset. Same reasoning as `data`:
# no trailing slash, so the symlink itself is ignored.
test_data/genomes

# --- secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this repository is a secret store, but credential-shaped files
# must never enter git even by accident.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The synthetic fixtures ARE committed: they are inputs to the test suite and
# must be byte-stable for assertions to mean anything. See
# docs/reproducibility.md ("Determinism").
#
# The `*.fasta` / `*.fna` globs above exist to keep the REAL dataset out, but
# they are path-blind and so also swallowed the synthetic alignments in
# `test_data/phylogeny/`. Those two files are generator output
# (`papipeline.testing.synthetic.write_alignments`, seeded, byte-stable) and
# tests read them from `test_data/phylogeny/`, so the suite was red in any
# worktree that lacked a stray on-disk copy. Un-ignore them here, which is what
# the comment above always intended. `test_data/genomes` stays ignored via the
# `test_data/genomes` rule above; this does not re-admit it.
!test_data/phylogeny/*.fasta
# `test_data/intermediate/gubbins/recombination.filtered_polymorphic_sites.fasta`
# is the same kind of committed fixture: stage 8's TEST input, produced by the
# deterministic generator in
# pa-artifacts/round11/dag/make_test_gubbins_fixture.py. Without this the TEST
# DAG has no input for stage 8 in a fresh worktree, which is the "stray on-disk
# copy" failure the rule above already documents for the phylogeny alignments.
!test_data/intermediate/gubbins/*.fasta

